{
  "dom_id": "step-q242921",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${7}^{3x}={9}^{6-x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{3x} & ={9}^{6-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{6-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)=(6-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =(6-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
